\begin{abstract}
A symmetric nondegenerate bilinear form and an alternating three-form do
not by themselves identify a Cayley algebra. We prove a sufficient quadratic
cross-product identity which does, including its exact normalization. In
dimension seven the identity is certified by 196 vector equalities. This
gives a small possible join from the already supplied F004 forms to a
concrete split Cayley structure, without repeating the large permutation
action acquisition. No coefficient, scalar or identity is evaluated here.
\end{abstract}

\section{The composition criterion}
Let $k$ have odd characteristic, let $V$ be seven-dimensional, let
$\beta:V\times V\to k$ be symmetric and nondegenerate, and let
$\tau:V^3\to k$ be alternating. Define $T:V\times V\to V$ uniquely by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:T}
 \beta(T(x,y),z)=\tau(x,y,z).
\end{equation}
Thus $T$ is bilinear and alternating. In particular
\begin{equation}\label{eq:orth}
 \beta(T(x,y),x)=\beta(T(x,y),y)=0.
\end{equation}
Here $\beta$ is a bilinear form, not the full polar form of a previously
normalized quadratic norm.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:composition}
Suppose $\kappa\in k^\times$ satisfies, for all $x,y\in V$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:double}
 T(x,T(x,y))=\kappa\bigl(\beta(x,y)x-\beta(x,x)y\bigr).
\end{equation}
Set $B=\kappa\beta$. On $\mathcal C=k\oplus V$ define
\begin{align}
 (a,x)(b,y)&=(ab-B(x,y),\ ay+bx+T(x,y)),\label{eq:product}\\
 N(a,x)&=a^2+B(x,x).\label{eq:norm}
\end{align}
This is an eight-dimensional unital composition algebra: $N$ is
nondegenerate, $N(1,0)=1$, and $N(XY)=N(X)N(Y)$. It is a Cayley algebra
and is split when $k$ is finite. The restriction of the quadratic norm to
$V$ is $\kappa\beta(x,x)$; its bilinear half-polar form is $B$, and its
full polar form is $2B$. The corresponding cross-product three-form is
$\kappa\tau$. No square root of $\kappa$ is required.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By alternation and~\eqref{eq:T},
\[
 \beta(T(x,T(x,y)),y)=-\beta(T(x,y),T(x,y)).
\]
Pairing~\eqref{eq:double} with $y$ therefore gives
\[
 \beta(T(x,y),T(x,y))
 =\kappa\bigl(\beta(x,x)\beta(y,y)-\beta(x,y)^2\bigr).
\]
Multiplying by $\kappa$ yields
\begin{equation}\label{eq:crossnorm}
 B(T(x,y),T(x,y))=B(x,x)B(y,y)-B(x,y)^2.
\end{equation}
The element $(1,0)$ is a two-sided unit. Nondegeneracy of $N$ follows
from that of $B$ and the assumption that the characteristic is not two.
Using~\eqref{eq:orth} and~\eqref{eq:crossnorm}, direct expansion gives
\begin{align*}
 N((a,x)(b,y))
 &=(ab-B(x,y))^2+B(ay+bx+T(x,y),ay+bx+T(x,y))\\
 &=a^2b^2+a^2B(y,y)+b^2B(x,x)+B(x,x)B(y,y)\\
 &=(a^2+B(x,x))(b^2+B(y,y)).
\end{align*}
Thus this is a unital composition algebra of dimension eight, namely a
Cayley algebra in the usual terminology. The classical composition-algebra
interpretation and Cayley-frame facts are the same retained inputs as in
the F004 octonion notes \cite{Wilson,Frames}.

One may also see alternativity directly. For a pure element $X=(0,x)$,
$X^2=(-B(x,x),0)$. The two sides of $X(XY)=X^2Y$, first for pure $Y$,
agree by~\eqref{eq:double}; the right alternative identity follows from
the same equation and the skew-symmetry of $T$. Bilinearity and the unit
extend these identities to arbitrary $X,Y$.

If $k$ is finite, every nondegenerate quadratic form of dimension at least
two represents every nonzero scalar. Indeed, after diagonalizing a binary
subform $au^2+bv^2$, the sets $\{au^2\}$ and $\{s-bv^2\}$ both have
$(|k|+1)/2$ elements and hence intersect. Apply this to $B(x,x)$ with
$s=-1$. Then $(1,x)$ is a nonzero isotropic vector of $N$, so the Cayley
algebra is split (the classical isotropic-norm characterization of the
split Cayley algebra). Finally
$B(T(x,y),z)=\kappa\tau(x,y,z)$ explains the three-form scale and all
factors of two in the statement.
\end{proof}

Nonzero $\tau$ alone is insufficient, and a zero $\kappa$ is not allowed.
Failure of this criterion for a supplied pair of forms is no assertion
about other possible forms, generation, the intersection property, or a
census candidate.

\section{The exact finite certificate}
Fix an ordered basis $e_0,\ldots,e_6$ of $V$, with coordinate rows. Write
$\beta(x,y)=x\mathbf B y^t$, and store $\tau_{ijk}$ for $i<j<k$ in
increasing lexicographic order. Its full tensor is obtained by alternation.
Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:coeff}
 T(e_i,e_j)_r=\sum_{k=0}^6\tau_{ijk}(\mathbf B^{-1})_{kr}.
\end{equation}
This is the right-row convention of the retained tensor helper, with no
transpose or action-order change.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:196}
Equation~\eqref{eq:double} holds identically if and only if it holds for
\[
 x\in\{e_i:0\le i<7\}\cup\{e_i+e_\ell:0\le i<\ell<7\},
 \qquad y\in\{e_j:0\le j<7\}.
\]
These are $28\cdot7=196$ vector equalities, or 1372 scalar equalities.
The possible scalar $\kappa$ is unique. It can be derived from the first
nonzero right-hand coefficient, then all 196 equalities must be checked.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Subtract the right-hand side of~\eqref{eq:double} from the left. The
result is homogeneous quadratic in $x$ and linear in $y$. Evaluation at
$x=e_i$ gives the coefficient of $x_i^2$. Subtracting the evaluations at
$e_i$ and $e_\ell$ from that at $e_i+e_\ell$ gives the coefficient of
$x_ix_\ell$. Evaluation on the $y$-basis gives every remaining linear
coefficient. No division by three, or interpretation of field traces,
is involved. In explicit mixed form the equation is
\[
 T(e_i,T(e_\ell,e_j))+T(e_\ell,T(e_i,e_j))
 =\kappa\bigl(\beta(e_i,e_j)e_\ell+\beta(e_\ell,e_j)e_i
                   -2\beta(e_i,e_\ell)e_j\bigr).
\]
Thus the argument works also in characteristic three.

The polynomial vector $R(x,y)=\beta(x,y)x-\beta(x,x)y$ is not zero.
Choose a vector $x$ of nonzero norm and a nonzero $y\in x^\perp$; such
vectors exist for a nondegenerate symmetric form in odd characteristic
and dimension seven. Then $R(x,y)=-\beta(x,x)y\ne0$. The coefficient
argument already proved means at least one of the displayed test values
of $R$ has a nonzero coordinate. If the corresponding coordinate of
$T(x,T(x,y))$ is $l$ and that of $R$ is $r\ne0$, any valid scalar must be
$\kappa=l/r$. Reject $\kappa=0$ and check every relation with that scalar.
One ratio or a capped subset is not a certificate.
\end{proof}

The complete output may record $\kappa$, $\kappa\mathbf B$, the
eight-by-eight quadratic-form matrix $\operatorname{diag}(1,\kappa\mathbf B)$,
its twice-matrix polar form, and all $8^3=512$ multiplication coefficients
from~\eqref{eq:product}. No coordinate search, nullspace solve, group order
or large permutation action is needed for this sufficient check.

\paragraph{Rescaling.}
If $\beta'=r\beta$ and $\tau'=s\tau$ with $r,s\ne0$, then
$T'=(s/r)T$, $\kappa'=(s^2/r^3)\kappa$, and
$\kappa'\beta'=(s/r)^2\kappa\beta$. Thus the recovered pure norm changes
by a square under such rescaling. The two resulting algebras are explicitly
isomorphic by $(a,x)\mapsto(a,(s/r)x)$. This observation does not replace
the actual identity check or assert uniqueness of the previously computed
invariant spaces.

\section{The faithful-model bridge, with its premises separated}
Let $M=\gen{M_1,M_2}\le\GL(V)$. Suppose the actual ordered generators
preserve the exact forms $\beta,\tau$. Nondegeneracy in~\eqref{eq:T}
then gives
\[
 T(xM_i,yM_i)=T(x,y)M_i.
\]
Consequently $\operatorname{diag}(1,M_i)$ preserves the product and norm
of Theorem~\ref{thm:composition}. Thus
\begin{equation}\label{eq:inclusion}
 M\ \le\ \Aut_k(\mathcal C).
\end{equation}
This is an actual inclusion proved by finite coefficient identities, not
an identification from matching group orders.

For the q11 application the already acquired faithful frame gives
$M\cong G_{\rm saved}$ on the exact two ordered matrices. Retain separately
the named model/order premise
\[
 |G_{\rm saved}|=|G_2(11)|=|\Aut_{\F_{11}}(\mathcal C)|,
\]
where the last equality uses that $\mathcal C$ is a split Cayley algebra.
Then~\eqref{eq:inclusion} and equality of finite orders imply
$M=\Aut_{\F_{11}}(\mathcal C)$. There is no new group-order call or
unproved equal-order-only recognition step. The order and named-Cayley
automorphism theorem are retained premises, not consequences of the
forms verifier. The normalization certificate alone does not certify them.

This supplies a possible concrete natural-Cayley-action join for the
eight-ordered-frame theorem \cite{Frames}. It need not first recover a
basis change to an unseen historical octonion tensor: the certified
$\mathcal C$ itself is a split Cayley algebra on the existing matrix
coordinates. Obtaining actual eight frames and all old-inventory
transports is still a separate finite obligation. No rank-five pair
classification, IP decision, candidate exclusion or census count follows
from this algebra identification alone.

